WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.117
NOT negates a Boolean expression.

00:00:02.117 --> 00:00:05.737
If an expression is TRUE, NOT makes it FALSE.

00:00:05.737 --> 00:00:08.862
If it is FALSE, NOT makes it TRUE.

00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:14.332
On this slide, we keep active records while excluding selected diagnoses.

00:00:14.332 --> 00:00:17.682
We can think of it as: the patient must be active,

00:00:17.682 --> 00:00:21.317
and the diagnosis must not belong to this excluded group.

00:00:21.317 --> 00:00:24.907
Parentheses help make the target of NOT obvious.

00:00:24.907 --> 00:00:27.142
We are not negating one value.

00:00:27.142 --> 00:00:32.247
We are negating the whole membership test.

00:00:32.247 --> 00:00:35.802
But there is an important SQL detail: NOT does not eliminate uncertainty.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:44.232
If an expression is UNKNOWN because it involves NULL, then NOT UNKNOWN is still UNKNOWN.

00:00:44.232 --> 00:00:49.947
Imagine a retail table with a membership status that can be Gold, Silver, Basic, or NULL.

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:55.797
If we ask for NOT Gold using an ordinary comparison, the NULL row does not automatically become “not

00:00:55.797 --> 00:01:03.377
Gold.” SQL does not know whether that missing value might actually have been Gold.

00:01:03.377 --> 00:01:04.472
The result can remain UNKNOWN.

00:01:04.472 --> 00:01:08.697
This matters because WHERE keeps only TRUE rows.

00:01:08.697 --> 00:01:11.692
So when you write NOT, ask two questions.

00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:14.367
First, what exact condition am I negating?

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:18.492
Second, can any values involved be NULL?

00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:23.572
Sometimes a more explicit predicate such as IS DISTINCT FROM communicates the rule better than a negated

00:01:23.572 --> 00:01:27.652
ordinary comparison. We will see that later.

00:01:27.652 --> 00:01:34.568
The main idea is to avoid treating missing information as if it were known information.
